    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\nFord Oakville Assembly Returns to Vehicle Production With F-Series Super Duty Line\n10 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nFord's very first Oakville-produced Super Duties are now heading to dealers, giving the Detroit-based automaker a third North American assembly plant for its biggest F-Series models. Oakville Assembly received in the ballpark of CAD 5 billion to get here, and there is a lot more going on here that meets the eye.\nBeyond the substantial reconstruction, the Canadian vehicle production complex had to bring together a workforce that spent years building very different vehicles. In the first instance, Oakville specialized in a wide range of Fords , Mercurys, and Edsels.\nThen came the pickup truck and commercial vehicle era, which lasted from the 1960s all the way to 2004. From the 1990s to 2007, said complex also built the Windstar, Freestar, and Monterey people carriers.\nThe wagon and crossover era started in the latter part of the 2000s, with nameplates including the Lincoln MKX, Ford Edge, Flex, MKT, and Nautilus. Edge production ended in May 2024, when the Oakville complex was idled in preparation for an electric vehicle pivot. That plan fell apart, with FoMoCo deciding on F-Series Super Duty workhorses instead.\nDearborn confirmed the switchover in July 2024. The media release published back then clearly states \"future multi-energy technology\" for the next generation of the Super Duty line, which is another way of saying that hybrid trucks or greater levels of electrification are on the menu. Whether that means an extended-range electric Super Duty is planned as well, we can't say for certain yet.\nPhoto: Ford Spreading F-Series Super Duty production across three sites will help North American dealers accommodate more configurations while shortening customer waiting times, particularly for commercial buyers.\nA contractor, a utility company, or an ambulance operator might need particular configurations rather than whichever truck happens to sit on a dealership lot, and those customers represent an important part of the Super Duty business in both the US and the Canadian market.\nIn preparation for Super Duty production, FoMoCo rebuilt the final assembly areas, paint operations, and body shops in just around two years. The American automaker has also added its first stamping facility in Canada. Helped by collaborative products, assembly line workers do their thing on a line that stretches approximately 1,000 feet (a little over 300 meters).\nAI-enabled cameras inspect the pickups as they move through Oakville Assembly, giving workers another way to identify potential quality concerns rather than relying exclusively on end-of-line inspections. As of November 2026, the facility is home to more than 2,000 employees. The mix between production and skilled trades hasn't been detailed by the Dearborn-based automaker.\nSkilled trades in this context stands for everything from electricians to millwrights, toolmakers, and robotics specialists. Think of them as the guys and gals who keep things operational. Production workers, meanwhile, handle assembly. Oakville Assembly received approval to ship its first Super Duty trucks two weeks ahead of schedule, and Ford claims the quality at Oakville matches that of Kentucky.\nEncouraging launch results, for sure, but sustained production and the NHTSA 's recall database will provide a much better indication of how consistently the new operation performs. In the first instance, Oakville Assembly promises up to 100,000 additional units of Super Duty production capacity per year.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord F-Series Ford F-Series Super Duty Ford Super Duty Ford production Canada\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Time Capsule 1962 Ford Thunderbird Ragtop Rocks Rare Factory Option, Original V8 Is Even Rarer",
